<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kenneth Kaunda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1924–2021</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kenneth Kaunda was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, from independence in 1964 until 1991. Born at Lubwa mission in northern Zambia, he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">United National Independence Party</a> in the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Central African Federation</a>. He made Zambia a one-party state in 1972, nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines and supported liberation movements in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Economic decline forced multiparty elections in 1991, which he lost to Frederick Chiluba.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda.jpg" alt="Kenneth Kaunda" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenneth_David_Kaunda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
